ready: 1
kind: rule
id: trust.rule.source-tier
display_id: RULE-TRUST-TIER
module: trust
title: Trust tier of a capture or memory
summary: >-
  Every capture gets a tier from who produced it, and a derived memory gets the lowest tier among the
  sources its claim depends on.
owner: crates/kernle-core/src/trust/tier.rs::tier_for
table:
  columns:
  - Source
  - Tier
  rows:
  - - The person's own typed words, or an owner command in an interactive terminal
    - principal
  - - The outcome of a tool the agent ran (success or failure, exit status, test counts)
    - first_party
  - - >-
      The agent's deliberate memory call, assistant messages, or model extraction during processing or
      sleep
    - agent_inferred
  - - >-
      Tool output text, file contents, web pages, other tools' results, and imports of unknown authorship
    - third_party
calculations:
- name: derived tier
  expr: >-
    lowest tier among the sources the derived claim depends on, ordered principal > first_party > agent_inferred
    > third_party
  notes: >-
    A source counts only when the extraction cites it as support for the claim. Context the model saw
    but did not cite does not raise or lower the tier.
- name: tier after owner review
  expr: principal when the owner accepts or edits the memory in review
default: Third-party, when the source cannot be identified
constraints:
- text: >-
    A tier is never raised by adding more citations; only owner review or an owner edit raises it.
  hard: true
rationale: |-
  Taking the highest tier among cited sources lets injected text borrow trust by citing the person's task message next to it. Taking the lowest tier means a claim is only as trusted as the weakest thing it stands on, so outside content cannot launder itself through a citation.

  Tool status and tool output are separate sources because whether a command succeeded is something the agent observed, while what a file or page printed may have been written by anyone.
